    "passage": "Squatters are people who search for uninhabited buildings to occupy without the owner’s knowledge. Many countries in the world allow squatting [[1]], granting the intruders so-called “squatters’ rights.” That is, if there is an unused space and the squatters do not impair the facilities, it is not considered a break-in. If squatters are discovered, the owners do not have the right to expel those [[2]] either—the case must be reported to the police, who will give the squatters a grace period of seven days to move out.\nSquatters in some countries can even legally claim title to the property and become the registered owners after [[3]] the building for a certain amount of time. In Australia, squatters can legitimize their ownership if they [[4]] a property for more than 12 years. In 2018, Sydney had a legal ruling in which Bill Gertos, an occupant who lived in a house sitting unattended for years, won the lawsuit against the previous owner to become the new property holder. The court [[5]] Gertos the ownership because he had maintained the house since 1998 and had spent more than $150,000 on repairs. This incident went viral and aroused heated discussions on squatters’ rights.",
